module tecs.audio.music

--[[
Songs played on the mixer's synthesizer voices: Standard MIDI Files on the
General MIDI synth, and register logs on emulated FM chips.

Two asset kinds load them through [`Assets`](tecs.assets.Assets), and a
[`Player`](tecs.audio.music.Player) plays a loaded song:

```nupp
local mixer <const> = tecs.audio.newAudio()
local font <const> = assert(tecs.audio.synth.loadSoundFont(mixer, "assets/music/FluidR3_GM.sf2"))
local song <const> = assert(app.assets:request(tecs.audio.music.midi.kind, "assets/music/theme.mid"))
local player <const> = assert(tecs.audio.music.midi.play(mixer, font, song, {group = "music"}))
song:release() -- the player holds the song for as long as it plays

local log <const> = assert(app.assets:request(tecs.audio.music.fm.kind, "assets/music/stage1.vgz"))
local chiptune <const> = assert(tecs.audio.music.fm.play(mixer, log, {group = "music"}))
log:release()
```

[`tecs.audio.music.midi`](tecs.audio.music.midi) reads `.mid` files, format
0, 1 and 2. [`tecs.audio.music.fm`](tecs.audio.music.fm) reads VGM, its
gzip-compressed form VGZ, and the DOSBox Raw OPL captures `.dro`, versions 1
and 2. Both parse in Nupp on the loader's readiness pump, a slice at a time, so
a large file loads across frames rather than stalling one.

## Playing

A player owns the voices it plays through, a synth for a MIDI song and one
chip for each chip a register log names, and frees them when it stops. It
holds the song handle it was given until then, so the caller releases its own
hold as soon as `play` returns. Every mixer `update` advances every player
made on that mixer, so a game that already drives its mixer drives its music
too, and keeps doing so while its world is paused.

The player schedules ahead on the voices' own clock: each update sends what
falls due within `lookahead` seconds of `now`, stamped with the time it is due
at, so the music keeps its timing whatever the frame rate does. After a stall
longer than the lookahead, the song slips rather than rushing to catch up: the
first event the stall made late plays at once, and everything after it keeps
its distance from it, rather than everything missed playing together. A stall
over a rest in the music changes nothing. A send the voice's bounded queue
refuses is retried on the next update.

## Looping, tempo and position

A song with a loop point loops forever unless `loop` says otherwise: a MIDI
file through a `loopStart` and `loopEnd` marker or controller 111, a VGM file
through its loop offset. A song without one plays once and then reports
`finished`. `setTempo` scales the speed: two plays twice as fast. On a MIDI
song that is the tempo; on a register log it is the rate the writes arrive at,
so notes keep their pitch but envelopes run at the chip's own speed.

`seek` moves to a time in the song. A MIDI player resets the synth and sends
the programs, controllers and pitch bends in force at that point; a register
log resets each chip and writes the last value of every register before it. A
note held across the point comes back on a MIDI song's next note on, and on a
chip with the envelope restarted.

## Reload

A song handle reloaded through `Assets.reload` advances its revision, and a
player watching it rebuilds itself on the new song at the position it had
reached. A register log whose chips changed makes new chips.

## Other formats

A player is driven through a [`Driver`](tecs.audio.music.Driver), which is all
a format module supplies. A game that has a format of its own can write one
and play it with `newPlayer`.
]]

local assets = require("tecs.assets")
local audio = require("tecs.audio")
local audiosynth = require("tecs.internal.audiosynth")

--- Seconds of song a player keeps scheduled ahead of the voice clock unless
--- asked otherwise: two or three frames' worth, which absorbs an ordinary
--- hitch.
local DEFAULT_LOOKAHEAD: number = 0.05

--- The range `lookahead` may take, in seconds.
local MIN_LOOKAHEAD: number = 0.005

local MAX_LOOKAHEAD: number = 2

--- Seconds after `now` a start, a seek or a slip lands at, so its first
--- events are scheduled rather than late.
local LEAD: number = 0.01

--- The shortest loop a player repeats, in seconds of song. A loop point that
--- leaves less than this is ignored, because repeating it would spin.
local MIN_LOOP: number = 0.001

--- The range `setTempo` takes.
local MIN_TEMPO: number = 0.01

local MAX_TEMPO: number = 100

--- Where a player stands.
export type State = "playing" | "paused" | "finished" | "stopped"

--- What a format module supplies to a [`Player`](tecs.audio.music.Player):
--- the voices a song plays on and the song's events, behind the operations a
--- player needs.
---
--- Song time is seconds from the start of the song at a tempo of one. Clock
--- time is seconds on the voices' own clock, which `now` reads. A player maps
--- one to the other with an anchor: song time `song` is clock time `clock`,
--- and `tempo` song seconds pass for each clock second.
export record Driver
    --- Read-only. Reports the song's length in seconds.
    duration: number

    --- Read-only. Reports where the song loops back to, in seconds, or nil for
    --- a song without a loop.
    loopStart: number?

    --- Read-only. Reports where a loop ends and the song goes back to
    --- `loopStart`, in seconds, or nil for the end of the song.
    loopEnd: number?

    --- Read-only. Reports the seconds a voice keeps sounding after the last
    --- event, for releases and reverb, before a finished song frees it.
    tail: number

    --- Read-only. Reports the voices' clock in seconds.
    now: function(): number

    --- Read-only. Sends every event due at or after the driver's cursor and
    --- before song time `to`, each at clock time
    --- `clock + (time - song) / tempo`, and returns the song time it reached:
    --- `to`, or the time of the first event a voice refused, which the next
    --- call starts from.
    schedule: function(to: number, song: number, clock: number, tempo: number): number

    --- Read-only. Reports the song time of the first event the cursor has not
    --- sent, or nil when every event has gone.
    pending: function(): number?

    --- Read-only. Moves the cursor back to song time `target` for a loop,
    --- after ending what still sounds at clock time `at`.
    jump: function(target: number, at: number): nil

    --- Read-only. Moves the cursor to song time `target` from anywhere,
    --- putting the voices in the state the song had there, at clock time `at`.
    seek: function(target: number, at: number): nil

    --- Read-only. Replaces the song with the payload of a reloaded handle,
    --- and returns false and a reason when it cannot.
    reload: function(payload: any): (boolean, string?)

    --- Read-only. Pauses every voice, which stops its clock.
    pause: function(): nil

    --- Read-only. Resumes every voice.
    resume: function(): nil

    --- Read-only. Sets the gain of every voice.
    setGain: function(gain: number): nil

    --- Read-only. Mutes or unmutes one part of the song: a MIDI channel or a
    --- chip, numbered from zero.
    setMuted: function(index: integer, muted: boolean): nil

    --- Read-only. Stops and frees every voice, over `fadeOut` seconds when
    --- that is given.
    stop: function(fadeOut: number?): nil
end

--- Configures a player. The format modules' `play` functions take it too.
export type PlayOptions = {
    --- Caller-writable. Selects the group the player's voices join, so the
    --- group's gain, mute and pause apply to them. Defaults to none.
    group: string?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the linear gain of the player's voices, before
    --- group and master gain, and defaults to one.
    gain: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Repeats the song from its loop point. Defaults to true
    --- when the song has one, and is ignored when it has none.
    loop: boolean?,

    --- Caller-writable. Scales the speed, from 0.01 to 100, and defaults to
    --- one.
    tempo: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the seconds the player schedules ahead, from
    --- 0.005 to 2, and defaults to 0.05.
    lookahead: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets where in the song playback starts, in seconds,
    --- and defaults to zero.
    start: number?
}

--- A song playing on voices of its own.
---
--- Made by `tecs.audio.music.midi.play`, `tecs.audio.music.fm.play` or
--- `newPlayer`, advanced by the mixer's `update`, and finished with `stop`.
--- Nothing here raises for a player that has stopped: every call on one
--- does nothing.
export record Player
    @private driver: Driver
    @private handle: assets.Handle?
    @private revision: integer
    @private state: State
    @private loop: boolean
    @private tempoScale: number
    @private lookahead: number
    @private cursor: number
    @private anchorSong: number
    @private anchorClock: number
    @private previousSong: number
    @private previousClock: number
    @private finishClock: number
    @private loopCount: integer
    @private completed: boolean
    @private failure: string?

    stop: function(exclusive self: Player, fadeOut: number?): nil
    pause: function(exclusive self: Player): nil
    resume: function(exclusive self: Player): nil
    status: function(borrows self: Player): State
    playing: function(borrows self: Player): boolean
    paused: function(borrows self: Player): boolean
    finished: function(borrows self: Player): boolean
    position: function(borrows self: Player): number
    duration: function(borrows self: Player): number
    seek: function(exclusive self: Player, seconds: number): nil
    setLoop: function(exclusive self: Player, loop: boolean): nil
    looping: function(borrows self: Player): boolean
    loops: function(borrows self: Player): integer
    setTempo: function(exclusive self: Player, scale: number): nil
    tempo: function(borrows self: Player): number
    setGain: function(exclusive self: Player, gain: number): nil
    setMuted: function(exclusive self: Player, index: integer, muted: boolean): nil
    reloadFailure: function(borrows self: Player): string?
    update: function(exclusive self: Player): boolean
end

--- Reports whether a player stands in `state`, as an expression the checker
--- does not read as a match over every state.
local function standing(borrows self: Player, state: State): boolean
    return self.state == state
end

--- Returns the song's loop as a start and an end, or nil when it has none
--- worth repeating.
local function loopOf(driver: Driver): (number?, number)
    local start = driver.loopStart
    local finish = driver.loopEnd or driver.duration
    if start == nil or finish - start < MIN_LOOP then
        return nil, driver.duration
    end

    return start, finish
end

--- Returns the clock time song time `song` is scheduled at.
local function clockOf(borrows self: Player, song: number): number
    return self.anchorClock + (song - self.anchorSong) / self.tempoScale
end

--- Moves the anchor, keeping the one it replaces for `position` while the
--- clock has not reached the new one.
local function anchor(exclusive self: Player, song: number, clock: number): nil
    self.previousSong = self.anchorSong
    self.previousClock = self.anchorClock
    self.anchorSong = song
    self.anchorClock = clock
end

--- Checks an options value against a range.
--- @raises string when the value is outside the range
local function within(name: string, what: string, value: number, low: number, high: number): nil
    if value ~= value or value < low or value > high then
        error(
            "tecs.audio.music." .. name .. ": " .. what .. " must be from " .. tostring(
                low
            ) .. " to " .. tostring(high) .. ", got " .. tostring(value),
            3
        )
    end
end

--- Raises unless play options are in range.
--- @raises string when `tempo`, `lookahead` or `start` is out of range
export function checkOptions(name: string, options: PlayOptions?): nil
    if options == nil then
        return
    end
    if options.tempo ~= nil then
        within(name, "tempo", options.tempo, MIN_TEMPO, MAX_TEMPO)
    end
    if options.lookahead ~= nil then
        within(name, "lookahead", options.lookahead, MIN_LOOKAHEAD, MAX_LOOKAHEAD)
    end
    if options.start ~= nil and (options.start ~= options.start or options.start < 0) then
        error("tecs.audio.music." .. name .. ": start must not be negative, got " .. tostring(options.start), 2)
    end
end

--- Starts a player on a driver and registers it with the mixer's update.
---
--- What the format modules' `play` functions end in, and what a game with a
--- format of its own calls with a driver it wrote.
---
--- @param mixer the mixer whose `update` advances the player
--- @param driver the song and its voices, which the player now owns
--- @param handle the song's handle, which the player retains until it stops
---     and watches for a reload, or nil for a song held some other way
--- @param options the loop, tempo, lookahead and start, or nil for the
---     defaults. `group` and `gain` are the driver's to apply.
--- @return the playing player
--- @raises string when an option is out of range
export function newPlayer(mixer: audio.Audio, driver: Driver, handle: assets.Handle?, options: PlayOptions?): Player
    checkOptions("newPlayer", options)
    local start = math.min((options and options.start) or 0, driver.duration)
    local clock = driver.now() + LEAD
    local player = new Player(
        driver = driver,
        handle = handle,
        revision = handle ~= nil and handle:revision() or 0,
        state = "playing",
        loop = not (options ~= nil and options.loop == false),
        tempoScale = (options and options.tempo) or 1,
        lookahead = (options and options.lookahead) or DEFAULT_LOOKAHEAD,
        cursor = start,
        anchorSong = start,
        anchorClock = clock,
        previousSong = start,
        previousClock = clock,
        finishClock = 0,
        loopCount = 0,
        completed = false,
        failure = nil
    )
    if handle ~= nil then
        handle:retain()
    end
    if start > 0 then
        driver.seek(start, clock)
    end
    local updates = audiosynth.updates[mixer]
    if updates == nil then
        updates = {}
        audiosynth.updates[mixer] = updates
    end
    updates[#updates + 1] = function(): boolean
        return player:update()
    end
    player:update()

    return player
end

--- Stops the song and frees its voices. Safe more than once.
---
--- @param self the player to stop
--- @param fadeOut seconds to fade the voices out over, or nil to cut them
function Player.stop(exclusive self: Player, fadeOut: number?): nil
    if standing(self, "stopped") then
        return
    end
    self.state = "stopped"
    self.driver.stop(fadeOut)
    local handle = self.handle
    if handle ~= nil then
        self.handle = nil
        handle:release()
    end
end

--- Pauses the song where it stands, notes held.
---
--- The voices pause, which stops their clock, so what was scheduled waits and
--- `resume` carries on exactly where the song was.
---
--- @param self the player to pause
function Player.pause(exclusive self: Player): nil
    if not standing(self, "playing") then
        return
    end
    self.state = "paused"
    self.driver.pause()
end

--- Resumes a paused song.
---
--- @param self the player to resume
function Player.resume(exclusive self: Player): nil
    if not standing(self, "paused") then
        return
    end
    self.state = "playing"
    self.driver.resume()
end

--- Reports where the player stands.
---
--- @param self the player to inspect
--- @return `"playing"`, `"paused"`, `"finished"` while a song that does not
---     loop rings out after its last event, or `"stopped"` once it has, or
---     once `stop` ran
function Player.status(borrows self: Player): State
    return self.state
end

--- Reports whether the song is playing.
---
--- @param self the player to inspect
--- @return true while playing, false paused, finished or stopped
function Player.playing(borrows self: Player): boolean
    return self.state == "playing"
end

--- Reports whether the song is paused.
---
--- @param self the player to inspect
--- @return true between `pause` and `resume`
function Player.paused(borrows self: Player): boolean
    return self.state == "paused"
end

--- Reports whether a song that does not loop has played to its end.
---
--- The voices keep sounding for the song's release tail, while `status` reads
--- `"finished"` and a `seek` can still play the song again, and then the
--- player stops itself and frees them. This stays true through that.
---
--- @param self the player to inspect
--- @return true once the last event has played, until a `seek` plays on
function Player.finished(borrows self: Player): boolean
    return self.completed
end

--- Returns how far into the song the voices are.
---
--- @param self the player to inspect
--- @return seconds of song at a tempo of one, from zero to the duration
function Player.position(borrows self: Player): number
    local now = self.driver.now()
    local song: number
    if now >= self.anchorClock then
        song = self.anchorSong + (now - self.anchorClock) * self.tempoScale
    else
        song = self.previousSong + (now - self.previousClock) * self.tempoScale
    end
    if song < 0 then
        return 0
    end

    return math.min(song, self.driver.duration)
end

--- Returns the song's length.
---
--- @param self the player to inspect
--- @return seconds at a tempo of one
function Player.duration(borrows self: Player): number
    return self.driver.duration
end

--- Moves playback to a time in the song.
---
--- A finished song plays again from there.
---
--- @param self the player to move
--- @param seconds the song time, clamped to the song
function Player.seek(exclusive self: Player, seconds: number): nil
    if standing(self, "stopped") then
        return
    end
    local target = math.max(0, math.min(seconds, self.driver.duration))
    local at = self.driver.now() + LEAD
    self.driver.seek(target, at)
    anchor(self, target, at)
    self.previousSong = target
    self.previousClock = at
    self.cursor = target
    if self.state == "finished" then
        self.state = "playing"
    end
    self.completed = false
end

--- Sets whether the song repeats from its loop point.
---
--- @param self the player to change
--- @param loop true to repeat, false to play on to the end
function Player.setLoop(exclusive self: Player, loop: boolean): nil
    self.loop = loop
end

--- Reports whether the song will repeat.
---
--- @param self the player to inspect
--- @return true when looping is on and the song has a loop point
function Player.looping(borrows self: Player): boolean
    local start = loopOf(self.driver)

    return self.loop and start ~= nil
end

--- Returns how many times the song has gone back to its loop point.
---
--- @param self the player to inspect
--- @return the count of loops scheduled so far
function Player.loops(borrows self: Player): integer
    return self.loopCount
end

--- Scales the playback speed from the point already scheduled.
---
--- @param self the player to change
--- @param scale from 0.01 to 100, where one is the song's own speed
--- @raises string when the scale is outside its range
function Player.setTempo(exclusive self: Player, scale: number): nil
    within("setTempo", "tempo", scale, MIN_TEMPO, MAX_TEMPO)
    anchor(self, self.cursor, clockOf(self, self.cursor))
    self.tempoScale = scale
end

--- Returns the speed scale.
---
--- @param self the player to inspect
--- @return the scale, one by default
function Player.tempo(borrows self: Player): number
    return self.tempoScale
end

--- Sets the gain of the player's voices, before group and master gain.
---
--- @param self the player to change
--- @param gain linear amplitude
function Player.setGain(exclusive self: Player, gain: number): nil
    if standing(self, "stopped") then
        return
    end
    self.driver.setGain(gain)
end

--- Mutes or unmutes one part of the song.
---
--- @param self the player to change
--- @param index a MIDI channel from 0 to 15, or a register log's chip from 0
--- @param muted true to silence it
function Player.setMuted(exclusive self: Player, index: integer, muted: boolean): nil
    if standing(self, "stopped") then
        return
    end
    self.driver.setMuted(index, muted)
end

--- Returns why the last reload of the song could not be played, if one
--- could not.
---
--- A reload the driver cannot take leaves the player on the song it had.
---
--- @param self the player to inspect
--- @return the reason, or nil
function Player.reloadFailure(borrows self: Player): string?
    return self.failure
end

--- Advances the player: what the mixer's `update` calls.
---
--- Ordinary game code does not call it. A test driving a player without a
--- mixer update does.
---
--- @param self the player to advance
--- @return false once the player has stopped, which drops it from the update
function Player.update(exclusive self: Player): boolean
    if standing(self, "stopped") then
        return false
    end
    local handle = self.handle
    if handle ~= nil and handle:revision() ~= self.revision then
        self.revision = handle:revision()
        local payload = handle:value()
        if payload ~= nil then
            local position = self:position()
            local reloaded, reason = self.driver.reload(payload)
            if reloaded then
                self.failure = nil
                self:seek(position)
            else
                self.failure = reason or "the reloaded song could not be played"
            end
        end
    end
    if standing(self, "paused") then
        return true
    end
    local driver = self.driver
    local now = driver.now()
    if standing(self, "finished") then
        if now >= self.finishClock + driver.tail then
            self:stop()

            return false
        end

        return true
    end
    local next = driver.pending()
    if next ~= nil and clockOf(self, next) < now then
        -- A stall outran the lookahead and an event is already late: play it
        -- now, and what follows it at its own distance, rather than rushing
        -- everything that was missed.
        anchor(self, next, now + LEAD)
        self.cursor = math.max(self.cursor, next)
    end
    local horizon = now + self.lookahead
    -- Bounded, so a loop shorter than one update's window cannot spin.
    for _ = 1, 64 do
        local loopStart, loopEnd = loopOf(driver)
        local looping = self.loop and loopStart ~= nil
        local finish = looping and loopEnd or driver.duration
        local target = self.anchorSong + (horizon - self.anchorClock) * self.tempoScale
        if target < finish then
            self.cursor = driver.schedule(target, self.anchorSong, self.anchorClock, self.tempoScale)

            return true
        end
        -- A loop ends before the events at its end, which belong to the next
        -- pass; the song's own end takes everything left, the note offs at
        -- its last tick included.
        local limit = looping and finish or math.huge
        local reached = driver.schedule(limit, self.anchorSong, self.anchorClock, self.tempoScale)
        if reached < limit then
            self.cursor = reached

            return true
        end
        self.cursor = finish
        local at = clockOf(self, finish)
        if looping and loopStart ~= nil then
            driver.jump(loopStart, at)
            anchor(self, loopStart, at)
            self.cursor = loopStart
            self.loopCount = self.loopCount + 1
        else
            self.state = "finished"
            self.completed = true
            self.finishClock = at
            if now >= at + driver.tail then
                self:stop()

                return false
            end

            return true
        end
    end

    return true
end
